content.text:
Recommend `cli/util.rs`, matching the existing `cli/migrate.rs` boundary.

```rust
// cli.rs
mod util;
pub use util::{UtilArgs, run_util};

pub enum Commands {
    Server,
    Data(DataArgs),
    Util(UtilArgs),
    Migrate(MigrateArgs),
}

// cli/util.rs
pub struct UtilArgs {
    command: UtilCommands,
}

enum UtilCommands {
    Latest { config: PathBuf },
    CopyLatestRaw {
        config: PathBuf,
        ingestion_key: Option<IngestionKey>,
    },
}

pub async fn run_util(args: UtilArgs) -> Response;
```

Move `latest`, `copy_latest_raw`, `IngestionResponse`, `IngestionLocation`, and `CopiedIngestion` into that module. Keep handler signatures, typed ingestion keys, configuration parsing, service calls, JSON schemas, and response serialization unchanged. The finite vocabulary owner becomes `UtilCommands`; remove these variants from `DataCommands`.

Add `Commands::Util` to `main.rs`, following its existing runtime/response pattern. A unified dispatcher would remove some repetition but expands the public `run_cli(DataArgs)` contract and introduces unrelated entrypoint restructuring. Leave it for a separate change.

Keeping everything in `cli.rs` is viable and yields fewer moved lines, but leaves utility-only response types and handlers mixed into pipeline code. The existing migration submodule makes extraction an established pattern.

Tests must account for command paths explicitly: `common/response_schema.rs` currently hardcodes `data`, and `cli_response_help.rs` only walks `DataArgs`. Prefer helpers accepting `&[&str]` command paths and enumerate both `DataArgs` and `UtilArgs`. Update latest/copy process invocations and add assertions that the old `data` paths fail while `util` paths retain their response contracts. Existing filesystem behavior coverage can remain unchanged.
